@translation labeled en project

#lg 14.1	proverb

@(A.14.1.1 = 1)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.2.5,]{6.2.5: UM 29-16-519 Seg. B l. 1}} Let the favour be repaid to him who repays a favour.

#lg 14.2	proverb

@(A.14.2.2 = 2)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.2.5,t625.h32]{6.2.5: NBC 8072}} May @wDN{Luma} grant prosperity to him  @varh[t6114.v1]{who speaks well of others } @vart[t6114.v2,]{@note{1 ms. has instead:} who has good things }.


#etcsl#altGrpo
#etcsl#alt t6114.v1 t6114.v2
#etcsl#altGrpc
#lg 14.3	proverb

@(A.14.3.3 = 3-4)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.16,t.6.1.16.c1]{6.1.16.c1}, @xref[t.6.2.5,]{6.2.5: UM 29-16-519 Seg. A ll. 7-8}} The farmer lifts his gaze to you @note{i.e. @wDN{Enlil}}: @q{“May @wDN{Enlil} look upon the city that he cursed.”}

#lg 14.4	proverb

@(A.14.4.5 = 5-6)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.15,t.6.1.15.b1]{6.1.15.b1}, @xref[t.6.1.16,t.6.1.16.c2]{6.1.16.c2}, @xref[t.6.2.5,]{6.2.5: UM 29-16-519 Seg. A ll. 9-10}} The herdsman lifts his gaze to you: @q{“May @wDN{Enlil} look upon the city that he cursed.”}

#lg 14.5	proverb

@(A.14.5.7 = 7-8)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.15,t.6.1.15.b2]{6.1.15.b2}, @xref[t.6.1.16,t.6.1.16.c3]{6.1.16.c3}, @xref[t.6.2.5,]{6.2.5: UM 29-16-519 Seg. A ll. 11-12}} The potter lifts his gaze to you: @q{“May @wDN{Enlil} look upon the city that he cursed.”}

#lg 14.6	proverb

@(A.14.6.9 = 9-11)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.03,t.6.1.03.149]{6.1.03.149}, @xref[t.6.1.16,t.6.1.16.c5]{6.1.16.c5}, @xref[t.6.1.22,]{6.1.22: ll. 172-175}, @xref[t.6.1.25,t.6.1.25.1]{6.1.25.1}} The day became cloudy but it did not rain. It rained, but not enough for people to undo their sandals. The @wWN{Tigris} was not surging at its inlet, so water did not fill the arable lands.

#lg 14.7	proverb

@(A.14.7.12 = 12-14)   @gap{3 lines fragmentary}   @gap{approx. 5-10 lines missing} 


#lg 14.15	proverb

@(B.14.15.1 = 1)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.01,t.6.1.01.68]{6.1.01.68}, @xref[t.6.1.25,t.6.1.25.6]{6.1.25.6}} You should drive them like pack-asses into a death-stricken city.

#lg 14.16	proverb

@(B.14.16.2 = 2-3)  The temple @wTN{E-babbar}, which @wRN{Me-silim} had built, was destroyed by @wDN{Nanni}, whose posterity had been cut off.

#lg 14.17	proverb

@(B.14.17.4 = 4)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.02,t.6.1.02.143]{6.1.02.143}} If a household has harmed an acquaintance, the matter should be investigated.

#lg 14.18	proverb

@(B.14.18.5 = 5-6)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.03,t.6.1.03.92]{6.1.03.92}, @xref[t.4.05.1,]{4.05.1: ll. 54-55}} @wDN{Enlil's} temple is a collecting (?) of wages (?); yet to reach out, to look with greedy eyes and to seize should be abominations there.

#lg 14.19	proverb

@(B.14.19.7 = 7)  Both the palace and the nether world require obedience from their inhabitants.

#lg 14.20	proverb

@(B.14.20.8 = 8-10)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.02,t.6.1.02.157]{6.1.02.157}, @xref[t.6.1.25,t.6.1.25.11]{6.1.25.11}, @xref[t.6.2.3,t623.h27]{6.2.3: UET 6/2 317}} A palace cannot ignore waste land. A barge cannot ignore straw. A freeborn man cannot ignore toil.

#lg 14.21	proverb

@(B.14.21.11 = 11-12)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.02,t.6.1.02.153]{6.1.02.153}} The palace is an ox; you should catch it by the tail. @wDN{Utu} is lord; you should fix your gaze on him.

